$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: safekeys/docs-hosting
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-27"
summary: >-
  Publish the generated specification docs to Cloudflare Pages at docs.safekeys.ai and
  dev-docs.safekeys.ai. Two docs audiences deploy as separate projects so internal architecture
  stays off the public site, and every deploy is built from the same pinned toolchain that CI
  validates.
$system: safekeys/system
$service: safekeys/site
intent: >-
  The specs are the source of truth, but until they are published they only exist on the machine of
  whoever ran `usm generate`. Hosting closes that gap: contributors get a browsable architecture and
  feature reference, and prospective users get the public half of it without the internal design
  notes alongside. The site is the adoption front door for the open core — the protocol is the
  product, so it has to be readable.

  Doing this on Cloudflare Pages keeps it free, global and dependency-free, and matches how USM
  itself is hosted, so there is one deploy pattern to understand rather than two. The important
  constraint is audience separation: the same generator produces both doc sets, and the difference
  between them is a filter. Getting that filter wrong publishes internal design notes, so the deploy
  must assert the public set contains no developer-only pages rather than trusting the flag.
status: built
flows:
  - id: publish-contributor-docs
    name: Publish the contributor docs
    description: Build the developer-audience docs and deploy them to dev-docs.safekeys.ai.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: validate
        target: Every .usm spec validates and the drift gate passes, so only a clean tree deploys.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: The pinned USM version generates both doc sets; the version is the same one CI gates on.
      - id: s3
        action: transform
        target: The static site is built, then its canonical URLs are corrected to our domain.
      - id: s4
        action: send
        target: The built directory is uploaded to the dev-docs Pages project.
      - id: s5
        action: observe
        target: >-
          The deploy is recorded with its commit; the previous deployment remains available for
          rollback.
  - id: publish-help-docs
    name: Publish the help docs
    description: Build the filtered public-audience docs and deploy them to docs.safekeys.ai.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: validate
        target: The public set is asserted to contain no developer-only pages before anything is uploaded.
      - id: s2
        action: transform
        target: The help site is built, then its canonical URLs are corrected to our domain.
      - id: s3
        action: send
        target: The built directory is uploaded to the help-docs Pages project.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: A failed audience assertion stops the deploy rather than publishing internal notes.
  - id: provision-hosting
    name: Provision the hosting
    description: The one-time Cloudflare and repository setup a new maintainer must perform.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: setup
        target: Two Pages projects are created, one per audience.
      - id: s2
        action: setup
        target: Custom domains are attached to each project, with DNS managed by Cloudflare.
      - id: s3
        action: setup
        target: Two repository secrets grant the workflow upload permission, scoped to Pages only.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: A missing secret fails the workflow loudly rather than silently skipping the deploy.
  - id: preview-docs-locally
    name: Preview the docs locally
    description: A maintainer reads both audiences on localhost before publishing.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: setup
        target: The generate target produces both doc sets.
      - id: s2
        action: observe
        target: The developer audience is served with live reload while specs are edited.
      - id: s3
        action: observe
        target: >-
          The help audience is served separately, so the filter can be inspected before a public
          deploy.
contracts:
  - id: audience-separation
    description: The public site must never contain pages intended only for contributors.
    must_have:
      - The help docs are a filtered set, not a copy
      - Architecture, design and data-model pages are absent from the public build
      - The deploy asserts the filter rather than trusting the flag
      - A failed assertion blocks publication
  - id: canonical-urls-are-ours
    description: Published pages must identify themselves as belonging to safekeys.ai.
    must_have:
      - The sitemap references the safekeys.ai domains, never a third party's
      - No published page advertises another project's canonical URL
      - The correction is applied on every build, because the generator rewrites it
  - id: deploy-uses-the-pinned-toolchain
    description: What is published is what CI validated.
    must_have:
      - The deploy job pins the same USM version as CI
      - The deploy runs only from a passing validation
      - Generated files are not committed, so the site cannot drift from the specs
  - id: cache-policy-preserves-freshness
    description: A redeploy must be visible immediately without losing asset caching.
    must_have:
      - HTML responses revalidate rather than being served stale from the edge
      - Hashed assets are cached immutably
      - Headers are written after the build, since each build clears the output directory
  - id: deploy-artifacts-carry-no-secrets
    description: What is uploaded is content only.
    must_have:
      - The upload token exists only in the workflow environment
      - No credential is embedded in the built site
      - The token is scoped to Pages deployment, not account-wide
  - id: rollback-is-possible
    description: A bad publish can be undone without a rebuild.
    must_have:
      - Each deployment is recorded against its commit
      - A previous deployment can be promoted
      - The workflow can be run manually for a deliberate republish
tests:
  - id: public-build-excludes-internal-pages
    flow: publish-help-docs
    setup:
      audience: help
    expect:
      - assertion: no architecture directory exists in the public output
      - assertion: no design directory exists in the public output
      - assertion: the assertion fails the job when an internal page is present
    contracts:
      - audience-separation
  - id: sitemap-uses-our-domain
    flow: publish-contributor-docs
    setup:
      artifact: sitemap.xml
    expect:
      - assertion: every sitemap location is under safekeys.ai
      - assertion: no location references a third-party domain
    contracts:
      - canonical-urls-are-ours
  - id: deploy-requires-valid-specs
    flow: publish-contributor-docs
    setup:
      spec_state: one spec edited without regenerating
    expect:
      - assertion: the drift gate fails before any upload occurs
    contracts:
      - deploy-uses-the-pinned-toolchain
  - id: missing-secret-fails-loudly
    flow: provision-hosting
    setup:
      secret: absent
    expect:
      - assertion: the workflow reports the missing credential
      - assertion: the deploy does not silently succeed with nothing published
    contracts:
      - deploy-artifacts-carry-no-secrets
  - id: cache-headers-present
    flow: publish-contributor-docs
    setup:
      artifact: _headers
    expect:
      - assertion: HTML is configured to revalidate
      - assertion: hashed assets are configured as immutable
    contracts:
      - cache-policy-preserves-freshness
  - id: published-site-serves-content
    flow: publish-help-docs
    setup:
      url: https://docs.safekeys.ai
    expect:
      - assertion: a known feature page returns its content
      - assertion: the site identifies itself as Safekeys, not another project
    contracts:
      - canonical-urls-are-ours
decisions:
  - id: cloudflare-pages
    decision: Host the docs and site on Cloudflare Pages, matching how USM is hosted.
    rationale: >-
      The domain is already on the account and DNS is already managed there, so adding a project
      needs no new provider. Pages is free at this scale, serves globally, and the deploy is a
      directory upload rather than a platform the project must learn. Using the same shape as USM
      means one deploy pattern to maintain across both projects.
    alternatives:
      - option: GitHub Pages
        rejected_because: >-
          The apex domain is already on Cloudflare, so DNS and TLS would be split across two
          providers for no benefit.
      - option: A container or VM serving the static site
        rejected_because: >-
          Adds an operating system to patch and a cache to configure, for content that is entirely
          static.
  - id: two-projects-per-audience
    decision: Publish the contributor and help docs as two separate Pages projects on separate subdomains.
    rationale: >-
      The two audiences differ by exactly one filter, and that filter is the only thing keeping
      internal architecture notes off the public site. Separate projects make the boundary
      structural rather than a flag on a build: a mistake in one cannot expose the other, and each
      can be republished or rolled back independently.
    alternatives:
      - option: One project serving both audiences under a path
        rejected_because: >-
          The internal pages would sit one directory away from the public ones, so the boundary
          would rest entirely on the build flag being right.
  - id: direct-upload-not-git-integration
    decision: >-
      Deploy by uploading the built directory from GitHub Actions, rather than connecting Pages to
      the repository.
    rationale: >-
      The site is generated from specs by a pinned toolchain, so the build must happen where that
      toolchain is pinned. Letting Pages build from the repository would put the generator version
      outside the repository's control and outside the CI that validates it, which is the specific
      drift this project already had to fix once.
    alternatives:
      - option: Cloudflare Pages Git integration
        rejected_because: >-
          Version selection and build steps would move to the provider's dashboard, unversioned and
          untested.
  - id: correct-canonical-urls-post-build
    decision: Rewrite the generated sitemap's domain after each build as a known workaround.
    rationale: >-
      The pinned generator writes a hardcoded sitemap hostname for the help and developer audiences
      with no configuration or environment override, so a published site would claim to belong to
      another project. Since the build is regenerated every run, the correction has to be applied
      every run; it is recorded as a workaround with an upstream report rather than treated as
      intended behaviour.
    alternatives:
      - option: Omit the sitemap until the generator allows configuring it
        rejected_because: Loses discoverability for the whole public site to correct one field.
      - option: Commit a corrected config file
        rejected_because: The generator rewrites it on every build, so the fix would not survive.
  - id: marketing-site-deploys-separately
    decision: Wire the docs deploy now and add the marketing site when it is built.
    rationale: >-
      The docs exist and build today; apps/safekeys is an empty directory. Splitting the workflows
      keeps the docs publishing unblocked and means the marketing build, which has a different
      toolchain and output, cannot break documentation deploys.
    alternatives:
      - option: Wait and deploy both together
        rejected_because: Withholds value that is already built, for no technical benefit.
implementation:
  primary: .github/workflows/docs-deploy.yml
  test_code: ""
  test_code_status: none
see_also:
  - safekeys/site
  - safekeys/deployment-packaging
